WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.495
Integers often control memory behavior.

2
00:00:02.575 --> 00:00:05.825
Lengths, indexes, offsets, capacities,
chunk sizes, page counts,

3
00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:09.553
and multiplication results can influence
allocation and access patterns.

4
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:12.027
Safe Rust prevents many memory-safety
failures,

5
00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:16.098
but it does not automatically decide
whether a size is reasonable or whether a value

6
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:17.603
from input should be trusted.

7
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:20.419
`usize` is the natural type for indexes
and memory sizes,

8
00:00:20.499 --> 00:00:22.893
but a value becoming `usize` does not make
it safe.

9
00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:25.596
A parsed protocol length, file size, query
parameter,

10
00:00:25.676 --> 00:00:29.381
or database value should be validated
before it becomes an allocation size or

11
00:00:29.461 --> 00:00:30.373
collection index.

12
00:00:30.453 --> 00:00:32.505
The context decides what range is allowed.

13
00:00:32.585 --> 00:00:34.751
Length and capacity are different
concepts.

14
00:00:34.831 --> 00:00:36.371
Length describes current data.

15
00:00:36.451 --> 00:00:38.275
Capacity describes allocated space.

16
00:00:38.355 --> 00:00:42.459
Offset, index, element count, byte count,
and record count are also different ideas.

17
00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:45.105
Mixing them can produce subtle bugs even
in safe code,

18
00:00:45.185 --> 00:00:47.522
especially near parsers and buffer
management.

19
00:00:47.602 --> 00:00:50.281
Allocation calculations deserve checked
arithmetic.

20
00:00:50.361 --> 00:00:53.781
Adding header size to payload size,
multiplying count by element size,

21
00:00:53.861 --> 00:00:57.738
or aligning a size upward can overflow
before the allocation call sees the value.

22
00:00:57.818 --> 00:01:01.922
Defensive code checks the calculation and
rejects impossible or excessive requests

23
00:01:02.002 --> 00:01:02.971
before allocating.

24
00:01:03.051 --> 00:01:06.871
Resource exhaustion is a security and
reliability concern even without memory

25
00:01:06.951 --> 00:01:07.578
corruption.

26
00:01:07.658 --> 00:01:11.363
Unbounded sizes can consume memory, CPU
time, disk space, or queue capacity.

27
00:01:11.443 --> 00:01:13.837
Apply explicit limits, document them, test
them,

28
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:16.027
and fail safely when a request exceeds
them.

29
00:01:16.107 --> 00:01:18.900
Safe Rust needs resource policy alongside
memory safety.
